   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 3.2. Increased capacity of member States to formulate and implement policies and strategies for creating decent work in the rural economy</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">1) As a result of bipartite consultative dialogues on the impact of COVID-19 on decent work and workers&apos; rights in the palm oil sector in 2020, the sectoral employers&apos; association GAPKI and the Palm oil trade union network JAPBUSI reached multifaceted agreements including on strict adherence to government COVID-19 protocols, intensifying efforts to ensure safe and continued operations of plantations to avoid lay-offs, enhancing focus on safe and healthy work environment, adopting the ILO&apos;s guidance on COVID-19 prevention and continued payment of festival allowance to the workers.

2) KAMIPARHO, a trade union federation active in the palm oil sector, successfully negotiated the conclusion of one collective bargaining agreement with clauses on prevention of violence and harassment against women at work and measures for preventing and mitigating COVID-19. 

3) In March 2020, the Directorate General of Labour Inspection under the Ministry of Manpower constituted the Strategic Compliance Planning Task Team for palm oil, consisting of 25 national and provincial labour inspectors. Based on the provincial strategic compliance plans (SCP) developed in the SCP workshop organized by the ILO in March 2020, provincial labour inspectors carried out 131 workplace inspection visits. The ILO&apos;s Guide on Labour Inspection in Indonesia&apos;s palm oil sector was also developed in the follow-up to the SCP and benefitted from the Task Team&apos;s technical inputs.

4) To address the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on the rural population, the Government through the Ministry of Villages launched various social assistance programs, including to strengthen the roles and capacities of BUMDES, the village-owned enterprises for rural economic development. 1) With support from the US Department of State (US-DOS), in March 2020, the ILO conducted the Strategic Compliance Planning workshop to facilitate stakeholder dialogue on improving compliance  in the palm oil sector. It contributed to strengthening the capacity of 25 national and provincial labour inspectors on strategic compliance planning; 131 inspections were conducted based on provincial plans developed in the workshop.
2) In 2020, the ILO facilitated dialogues between the sectoral employers&apos; association and the Palm oil trade union network on COVID-19 impact on workers&apos; rights, resulting in multifaceted agreements on OSH, allowance payment and safe and continued business operations. 
3) In 2020, the ILO conducted a study on freedom of association and collective bargaining in the palm oil sector. The Network adopted the study&apos;s recommendations in its 2021 workplan. The ILO also developed a policy brief capturing recent labour law amendments and COVID-19 impact to supplement the study.
4) In 2020-21, the ILO conducted capacity building trainings for the Network&apos;s trade union federation members, including on collective bargaining, OSH, gender issues and preventing workplace violence and harassment, resulting in drafting of 15 new CBAs and signing of one of them.
5) In August 2021, supported by the US-DOS, the ILO conducted a 2-week Virtual Academy on decent work in Indonesia&apos;s rural economy covering topics such as elimination of child labour and forced labour, non-discrimination, OSH, social protection, social dialogue, labour law compliance, and gender and youth issues. It was attended by 105 participants representing ministries, employers, unions and civil society.
6) The ILO and the Ministry of Villages collaborated to build capacity of 30 representatives of village-owned enterprises through trainings on management and digital marketing.</narrative>
   </description>
